                <head>343. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On May 1, 1962, President Kennedy chaired a White House meeting on the Dominican
                    situation according to the President’s Appointment Book. (Kennedy Library)
                    Present were Under Secretary of State McGhee, Assistant Secretary of State for
                    Inter-American Affairs Edwin Martin, Ambassador to the Dominican Republic John
                    Bartlow Martin, Rafael Pico, temporary U.S. coordinator of economic aid to the
                    Dominican Republic, Teodoro Moscoso and Newell Williams of AID, [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">text not declassified</hi>] of CIA, and Ralph Dungan of
                    the White House staff. No formal record of the meeting was kept. During the
                    meeting, which lasted from 4:30 to 5:27 p.m., the President inquired about the
                    Dominican sugar quota, the upcoming Dominican elections, the activities of the
                    recently established MAAG in Santo Domingo, and the chances of a military coup
                    in the Dominican Republic. He asked that the Dominican police force receive U.S.
                    instruction in riot control techniques. (Martin, Overtaken by Events, pages
                    121-122)</p>
